A||||3||zeusrekning||Quest on engine storage.||||1||
Z||000000||zeusrekning||11-12-2000||08:56 PM||||I am going to store the 87 2.5 out of my new coupe as a back up engine for my present coupe. What do i need to do before i bag it up and store it?<BR>Will be taken out of car before storage?<BR>I think i have a rod knock so it may not be stored long <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||11-12-2000||10:30 PM||||I don't know what the exact name is but a friend had a V-8 he stored for a few years on an engine stand. He sprayed it with a spray-on oil. I know he got it at NAPA. Then he covered it with heavy plastic. It was like the same type you find sprayed on tools and engine parts that come in plastic. It suppose to stop metal parts from corroding.<BR>If I can get ahold of him I'll ask him the name of it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-12-2000||11:01 PM||||Run the car with some Sta-bil or other fuel storgage stabilizer in it before taking out the motor. That will help protect fuel system parts.<P>pull all the plugs and fog it with some teflon loaded spray oil then hand rotate for one turn to spread the oil on the piston walls. fog it again for good measure and put the plugs back in. If you're storing inside fog the TBI lightly as well.<P>If it's kept outside you MUST make sure absolutly no water enters the intake. You must also provide some ventilation to prevent condensation buildup. The best thing is to remove the TBI unit and bolt a solid plate over the TBI mount. (and yes use a little RTV silicon under the plate to seal it.) Also make sure all the vacuum ports are capped. Remove the alternator as well. thinly coat all the belt pulleys running surfaces with heavy grease.<P>I'd also remove the flywheel and coat the crank end with grease.<P>It's absolutly critical no water runs into the engine. You also need to keep the DIS ingition parts dry. They won't like constant soaking either. It'll cause corosion in the terminals.||||reg||1
Z||000003||prof bobo||11-13-2000||11:13 AM||||It's like putting a boat up for the winter.  I just finished this task with 4 watercraft.<BR>Ogre's hit the major points, BE SURE THE BLOCK IS DRAINED DRY OF ANY WATER.  This means you'll have to take out the drain plugs in the block (usually 3/8" NPT plugs near the bottom of the block).  I usually drain mine, blow it out with compressed air and then dribble a little bit of RV antifreeze (the pink stuff) to ensure I've driven all the water out.  <P>If you are able to run the engine, the best thing to do is fog it through the intake, while it's running at fast idle until you kill it.<P>Oh, and you'll want to plug up the exhaust too to keep out moisture and critters.||||reg||1
